            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Welcome to C#!</h2>
                    <p>Welcome to your journey into C# programming! Whether you're completely new to programming or coming from another language, this course will give you a solid foundation in one of the most versatile and powerful programming languages in use today.</p>
                    
                    <p>In this first lesson, we'll explore what C# is, understand the .NET ecosystem, and get your development environment set up so you're ready to start coding.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is C#?</h2>
                    <p>C# (pronounced "C-sharp") is a modern, object-oriented programming language developed by Microsoft. It was created by Anders Hejlsberg and first released in 2000 as part of Microsoft's .NET initiative.</p>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Fun Fact</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>The name "C#" was inspired by musical notation. In music, a sharp (♯) raises a note by a semitone, suggesting that C# is an enhancement of the C language family.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Key Characteristics of C#</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Object-Oriented:</strong> Everything in C# revolves around objects and classes, making it easy to model real-world concepts in code.</li>
                        <li><strong>Type-Safe:</strong> C# catches many errors at compile time, before your code even runs, helping you write more reliable software.</li>
                        <li><strong>Modern:</strong> The language is continuously updated with new features that make coding more efficient and expressive.</li>
                        <li><strong>Cross-Platform:</strong> With .NET, your C# code can run on Windows, macOS, Linux, and even mobile devices.</li>
                        <li><strong>Versatile:</strong> Build web applications, desktop apps, mobile apps, games, cloud services, and more.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is .NET?</h2>
                    <p>.NET (pronounced "dot net") is a free, open-source development platform created by Microsoft. Think of it as the foundation that powers your C# applications.</p>

                    <h3>The .NET Ecosystem</h3>
                    <p>.NET provides everything you need to build applications:</p>

                    <div class="concept-cards">
                        <div class="concept-card">
                            <h4>Runtime (CLR)</h4>
                            <p>The Common Language Runtime executes your code, manages memory, and handles security.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="concept-card">
                            <h4>Base Class Library</h4>
                            <p>A huge collection of pre-built code for common tasks like file I/O, networking, and data structures.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="concept-card">
                            <h4>Frameworks</h4>
                            <p>Specialized libraries for building specific types of applications (web, desktop, mobile).</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="concept-card">
                            <h4>Tools</h4>
                            <p>The SDK, CLI tools, and IDE support for developing, testing, and deploying applications.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>.NET Versions</h3>
                    <p>You might hear about different versions of .NET:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>.NET Framework:</strong> The original Windows-only version (legacy).</li>
                        <li><strong>.NET Core:</strong> The cross-platform rewrite (versions 1.0-3.1).</li>
                        <li><strong>.NET 5+:</strong> The unified platform going forward. We use .NET 10 in this course.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="info-box success">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Recommendation</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Always use the latest .NET version for new projects. As of this course, that's .NET 10, which includes C# 13 with all the latest language features.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What Can You Build with C#?</h2>
                    <p>One of the best things about learning C# is its versatility. Here's what you can create:</p>

                    <div class="use-cases">
                        <div class="use-case">
                            <div class="use-case-icon">🌐</div>
                            <h4>Web Applications</h4>
                            <p>Build modern web apps with ASP.NET Core, Blazor, and web APIs.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="use-case">
                            <div class="use-case-icon">🖥️</div>
                            <h4>Desktop Applications</h4>
                            <p>Create Windows apps with WPF, WinForms, or cross-platform apps with MAUI.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="use-case">
                            <div class="use-case-icon">📱</div>
                            <h4>Mobile Apps</h4>
                            <p>Build iOS and Android apps with .NET MAUI or Xamarin.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="use-case">
                            <div class="use-case-icon">🎮</div>
                            <h4>Games</h4>
                            <p>Create games with Unity, the world's most popular game engine.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="use-case">
                            <div class="use-case-icon">☁️</div>
                            <h4>Cloud Services</h4>
                            <p>Build microservices, serverless functions, and cloud-native applications.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="use-case">
                            <div class="use-case-icon">🤖</div>
                            <h4>AI & Machine Learning</h4>
                            <p>Integrate ML models with ML.NET and work with AI services.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Setting Up Your Development Environment</h2>
                    <p>Before we can start coding, we need to set up our development environment. You'll need two things:</p>

                    <h3>1. Install the .NET SDK</h3>
                    <p>The .NET SDK (Software Development Kit) includes everything you need to build and run C# applications.</p>

                    <ol class="steps-list">
                        <li>
                            <strong>Download .NET SDK:</strong>
                            <p>Visit <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download" target="_blank">dotnet.microsoft.com/download</a> and download the latest .NET SDK for your operating system.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Run the installer:</strong>
                            <p>Follow the installation wizard. The default options are fine for most users.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Verify installation:</strong>
                            <p>Open a terminal or command prompt and run:</p>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet --version</code></pre>
                            <p>You should see the version number (e.g., <code>10.0.100</code>).</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>2. Choose an IDE or Code Editor</h3>
                    <p>An IDE (Integrated Development Environment) makes writing code much easier with features like syntax highlighting, code completion, and debugging.</p>

                    <div class="tool-options">
                        <div class="tool-option recommended">
                            <div class="tool-badge">Recommended</div>
                            <h4>Visual Studio Code</h4>
                            <p>Free, lightweight, and cross-platform. Perfect for beginners.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Download from <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com" target="_blank">code.visualstudio.com</a></li>
                                <li>Install the "C# Dev Kit" extension</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="tool-option">
                            <h4>Visual Studio</h4>
                            <p>Full-featured IDE for Windows (Community edition is free).</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Best debugging experience</li>
                                <li>Great for larger projects</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="tool-option">
                            <h4>JetBrains Rider</h4>
                            <p>Powerful cross-platform IDE (paid, with free trial).</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Excellent refactoring tools</li>
                                <li>Great performance</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Your First Look at C# Code</h2>
                    <p>Let's take a sneak peek at what C# code looks like. Don't worry about understanding everything yet – we'll cover each concept in detail in upcoming lessons.</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// This is a simple C# program
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Print a greeting to the console
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, future C# developer!");
        
        // Ask for the user's name
        Console.Write("What's your name? ");
        string name = Console.ReadLine();
        
        // Greet the user
        Console.WriteLine($"Nice to meet you, {name}!");
    }
}</code></pre>

                    <p>Let's briefly look at what's happening:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>using System;</code> – Imports functionality we need</li>
                        <li><code>class Program</code> – Defines a container for our code</li>
                        <li><code>static void Main()</code> – The entry point where our program starts</li>
                        <li><code>Console.WriteLine()</code> – Displays text on the screen</li>
                        <li><code>string name</code> – Creates a variable to store text</li>
                        <li><code>$"..."</code> – String interpolation (inserting variables into text)</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Note</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>In modern .NET (6+), you can write simpler "top-level" programs without all the ceremony. We'll explore both styles in the next lesson.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                    <div class="takeaways">
                        <LessonTakeaway>C# is a modern, type-safe, object-oriented language developed by Microsoft.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>.NET is the platform that runs C# applications and provides libraries and tools.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>C# can be used to build web, desktop, mobile, game, and cloud applications.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>You need the .NET SDK and a code editor to start developing.</LessonTakeaway>
                    </div>
                </section>

